DREAM RITUALS
You may wish to use a ritual as a way of physically depicting an inner attitude
change that a dream calls for. The best rituals are physical, solitary, and silent.
Approach your ritual with consciousness and reverence.
Years ago I had a dream about root vegetables that were jammed into one
small clay pot, strangling because ofl ack of air and space. In the dream I care-
fully separated them and put them into their own pots. At the
time of the dream, my twenty-seven-year-old son and I were
Detachment liberates the heart Posed y y
involved in a separation process that was painful to us both.
from the past and from the
Each of us was acting out the hurt and rejection we felt be-
future. It gives us the freedom to
cause ofo ur long-time enmeshment.
be who we are, loving others for S
When After having the dream, I decided to photograph root
vegetables, both together and apart. For several weeks I col-
-MARION WOODMAN
lected vegetables with the most tangled roots at the local
farmers' market and photographed them against a black vel-
vet background. As I made the prints and looked at both the individual and
the paired vegetables, I realized I was ritualizing the dream. It was certainly
time for me to extricate my roots from my son's and to let him "breathe" in
his own container. Several months later, he prepared to move three thousand
miles from home.

HEALING THE MOTHER-DAUGHTER SPLIT | 101
Sara, a thirty-five-year-old woman in my women's dream group, had a
dream about a beautiful, full-length red wool cape that represented her femi-
ninity. She had been grieving the stillbirth of her baby a year previous to the
dream and knew that the cape represented an emerging aspect of her healed
feminine nature. When the other women in the group suggested that she ritu-
alize the dream, she went right out and bought a pattern and red cashmere wool
fabric to make the cape.
